"""Mastery Edition lesson source. Each module has a business reason, mechanism, runnable/demonstrative
example, an explicitly scoped expected result, diagnostics, professional practice and 2 worked checks.
Content examples use the intentionally small, fictional mastery_orders.csv unless noted otherwise.
"""

def L(title, objective, why, how, example, result, tip, trap, q1, a1, q2, a2, recap, kind='code'):
    return dict(title=title, objective=objective, why=why, how=how, example=example,
                result=result, tip=tip, trap=trap, checks=[{'question':q1,'solution':a1}, {'question':q2,'solution':a2}], recap=recap, kind=kind)

SQL = [
L('SQL foundations and relational thinking','Return a trustworthy transaction-level extract.',
'A retail manager asks which orders were completed. A database stores repeated transactions consistently, and SQL retrieves the precise rows required for the decision.',
'A table has a row grain (one order here), typed columns, and an identifying key. SELECT chooses columns, FROM chooses the table, WHERE restricts rows, and ORDER BY makes presentation deterministic. The engine parses SQL, builds a query plan, and evaluates it on stored rows.',
"SELECT order_id, customer, quantity, unit_price\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'Completed'\nORDER BY order_id\nLIMIT 3;",
'order_id | customer | quantity | unit_price\n1001     | Amina    | 2        | 150000\n1002     | Kola     | 5        | 8000\n1004     | Ngozi    | 4        | 18000',
'List only required columns; ORDER BY before LIMIT makes the sample reproducible.',
'Without ORDER BY, LIMIT does not guarantee which three rows are returned.',
'Why is order_id a more reliable identifier than customer?', 'Several orders can belong to one customer; order_id uniquely identifies each transaction.',
'Change the query to return the two newest completed orders.',"Keep the same SELECT and WHERE; replace ORDER BY with order_date DESC, order_id DESC and LIMIT 2. Expected IDs: 1006, 1005.",
'Identify table grain; SELECT specific columns; WHERE filters; ORDER BY makes results stable.'),
L('Filtering, Boolean logic and sorting','Distinguish row eligibility from display order.',
'Teams often ask for one region, one status, and a minimum transaction size; incorrect AND/OR logic quietly changes reported sales.',
'WHERE evaluates a Boolean condition per row, before grouping. AND binds more tightly than OR, so brackets communicate the intended condition. Text equality and numeric comparisons differ; NULL requires IS NULL, not = NULL.',
"SELECT order_id, region, quantity * unit_price AS gross_value\nFROM orders\nWHERE status='Completed' AND region IN ('East','West')\n  AND quantity * unit_price >= 40000\nORDER BY gross_value DESC, order_id;",
'order_id | region | gross_value\n1001     | East   | 300000\n1005     | West   | 150000\n1002     | West   | 40000',
'Filter timestamps with an inclusive start and exclusive next-day boundary for portable date ranges.',
'WHERE region=\'East\' OR region=\'West\' AND status=\'Completed\' also admits returned East orders.',
'Which operator handles several acceptable regions?', 'IN (\'East\',\'West\') is clearer than repeated OR expressions.',
'Find completed orders whose quantity is less than 3.',"Use WHERE status='Completed' AND quantity < 3. Expected order IDs: 1001, 1005, 1006.",
'Filter before aggregation; parenthesize mixed conditions; sort using an explicit tie-break.'),
L('Aggregations and KPI definitions','Calculate net sales with a clear numerator and denominator.',
'A performance dashboard must reconcile to finance: one returned order must not inflate completed-order revenue.',
'GROUP BY forms one group per region; SUM reduces rows to a metric. WHERE removes unqualified transactions before aggregation; HAVING filters completed groups. Financial reporting must define whether revenue includes tax, discounts, refunds and returns.',
"SELECT region, COUNT(*) AS completed_orders,\n       SUM(quantity*unit_price) AS revenue\nFROM orders\nWHERE status='Completed'\nGROUP BY region\nORDER BY revenue DESC;",
'region | completed_orders | revenue\nEast   | 2                | 316000\nWest   | 2                | 190000\nNorth  | 1                | 72000\nTOTAL  | 5                | 578000',
'Validate the total with an independent ungrouped SUM query (should be 578000).',
'COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(column) ignores NULLs. Also never SUM a rate such as margin percentage.',
'How is profit calculated in this example?', 'SUM(quantity*(unit_price-unit_cost)) on completed orders = 135000.',
'Return only regions with completed revenue above 180000.', 'Append HAVING SUM(quantity*unit_price)>180000: East and West remain.',
'Know each KPI definition; group at the requested grain; reconcile aggregates to source totals.'),
L('Joins and relationship cardinality','Enrich facts without accidentally duplicating revenue.',
'Order records may hold a customer_id while the customer segment sits in a separate table; analyses need reliable dimension joins.',
'JOIN matches related keys. One-to-many means a customer can have many orders; many-to-many or duplicate dimension keys can multiply rows. An INNER JOIN keeps matches, while LEFT JOIN keeps all rows on the left and flags unmatched reference data.',
"SELECT o.order_id, c.segment, o.quantity*o.unit_price AS revenue\nFROM orders AS o\nLEFT JOIN customers AS c ON o.customer_id=c.customer_id\nWHERE o.status='Completed'\nORDER BY o.order_id LIMIT 3;",
'order_id | segment  | revenue\n1001     | Consumer | 300000\n1002     | Business | 40000\n1004     | Business | 72000',
'Check that the sum before and after a many-to-one lookup join is still 578000.',
'Do not join on non-unique names or forget the ON clause; check row counts and missing keys.',
'Why prefer LEFT JOIN when auditing master data?', 'It retains unmatched orders so bad or missing customer IDs remain visible.',
'Write a missing-reference check.', 'SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders o LEFT JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id=c.customer_id WHERE c.customer_id IS NULL; returns 0 for the training data.',
'Join on stable keys; know cardinality; test unmatched keys and duplicated totals.'),
L('NULLs, CASE and data quality','Translate raw statuses into explicit business categories.',
'Reports routinely distinguish completed, returned and pending transactions, plus unknown values that must not silently fall into a valid bucket.',
'NULL means unknown or missing, not zero or blank text. CASE evaluates WHEN branches in order; COALESCE chooses the first non-NULL input. A quality label should be mutually exclusive and include an ELSE branch.',
"SELECT order_id, status,\n CASE WHEN status='Completed' THEN 'Recognised'\n      WHEN status='Returned' THEN 'Reversal'\n      ELSE 'Review' END AS status_group\nFROM orders ORDER BY order_id LIMIT 4;",
'order_id | status    | status_group\n1001     | Completed | Recognised\n1002     | Completed | Recognised\n1003     | Returned  | Reversal\n1004     | Completed | Recognised',
'Inspect DISTINCT status values before writing a CASE mapping.',
'WHERE status <> \'Completed\' does not capture NULL statuses; test IS NULL explicitly.',
'How many Returned rows are in the training dataset?', "One. Filter with WHERE status='Returned', and order 1003 is returned.",
'Flag an unexpected status without discarding it.',"CASE WHEN status IN ('Completed','Returned') THEN 'Known' ELSE 'Investigate' END handles other and NULL values.",
'Distinguish NULL from zero; define category mappings; inspect unmapped exceptions.'),
L('CTEs and layered SQL','Separate row-level business logic from aggregation.',
'Auditors and colleagues should be able to trace a KPI back to its qualifying transactions instead of interpreting a single oversized query.',
'A Common Table Expression (WITH) names an intermediate result within a single statement. Build detail-level calculated columns in one step, aggregate in another; most databases decide whether to inline or materialize CTEs based on the optimizer.',
"WITH completed_sales AS (\n SELECT region, quantity*unit_price AS revenue\n FROM orders WHERE status='Completed'\n)\nSELECT region, SUM(revenue) AS total_revenue\nFROM completed_sales GROUP BY region ORDER BY total_revenue DESC;",
'region | total_revenue\nEast   | 316000\nWest   | 190000\nNorth  | 72000',
'Use naming that describes the transformation: validated_orders, completed_sales, monthly_kpis.',
'A CTE is not automatically cached or faster; benchmark the actual execution plan.',
'Where should the Completed status restriction be placed?', 'Inside completed_sales, so downstream consumers inherit the same business rule.',
'How can you inspect the intermediate detail?', 'Temporarily replace the final aggregate with SELECT * FROM completed_sales and review its five rows.',
'One CTE per logical transformation; readable aliases; verify intermediates before final sums.'),
L('Window functions and ranking','Rank customers while preserving transaction detail.',
'Top-customer reporting and monthly running totals need calculations that do not collapse the original records.',
'Unlike GROUP BY, OVER computes within a window of rows while returning each selected row. PARTITION BY creates independent groups; ORDER BY defines sequence. ROW_NUMBER chooses one row per partition only when the sort has a reliable tie-break.',
"SELECT customer, order_id, order_date,\n ROW_NUMBER() OVER(\n PARTITION BY customer ORDER BY order_date DESC, order_id DESC\n ) AS recency_rank\nFROM orders WHERE status='Completed'\nORDER BY customer, recency_rank;",
'customer | order_id | recency_rank\nAmina    | 1001     | 1\nIdris    | 1005     | 1\nKola     | 1002     | 1\nNgozi    | 1006     | 1\nNgozi    | 1004     | 2',
'Use ROW_NUMBER for deterministic latest-row selection; use RANK/DENSE_RANK only when ties carry meaning.',
'Window ORDER BY and final output ORDER BY serve different purposes. Missing tie-breaks make latest-row logic unstable.',
'What is the latest Completed order for Ngozi?', 'Order 1006; 2026-02-18 sorts after 2026-02-09.',
'Write the rule to return only the latest row per customer.', 'Wrap the query in a CTE named ranked, then SELECT * FROM ranked WHERE recency_rank=1.',
'WINDOW = partition + ordering + optional frame; row counts remain unchanged.'),
L('Date analysis and period-over-period metrics','Produce monthly series with correct chronological order.',
'Leadership needs January and February comparisons; grouping only by month name mixes years and creates misleading trends.',
'SQLite strftime returns a sortable YYYY-MM month key. Filter timestamps at the desired grain, group by year-month, and use LAG for a prior period. The dialect matters: use DATE_TRUNC in other systems, not SQLite.',
"SELECT strftime('%Y-%m',order_date) AS month,\n SUM(quantity*unit_price) AS revenue\nFROM orders WHERE status='Completed'\nGROUP BY strftime('%Y-%m',order_date) ORDER BY month;",
'month   | revenue\n2026-01 | 340000\n2026-02 | 238000',
'Build a calendar dimension for continuous reporting; no order in a month is not always a zero-sales month.',
'Beware string-sorted month names and using current-month partial data against a complete prior month.',
'Calculate February month-on-month change.', '(238000-340000)/340000 = -30%. LAG returns NULL for January without a prior row.',
'Why is YYYY-MM safer than grouping by MonthName?', 'It preserves both year context and sort order across Dec/Jan.',
'Use year-aware period keys; compare complete, like-for-like periods; document date boundaries.'),
L('Performance and validation','Improve query execution without breaking metric integrity.',
'A fast incorrect report is worse than a slower audited one; reliability comes before indexing and speed work.',
'Start with expected row counts, distinct keys, nulls and reconciliations. EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN describes access paths and joins. Indexes can improve selective lookups but add storage and write overhead; on small training datasets a full scan may be optimal.',
"SELECT COUNT(*) AS row_count,\n COUNT(DISTINCT order_id) AS unique_orders,\n SUM(CASE WHEN status='Completed'\n          THEN quantity*unit_price ELSE 0 END) AS revenue\nFROM orders;",
'row_count | unique_orders | revenue\n6         | 6             | 578000',
'Keep the validation query beside the production query; rerun both after any optimization.',
'EXPLAIN output is platform-specific, and an index is not inherently better for tiny tables.',
'Which measure exposes duplicate order keys?', 'COUNT(*) minus COUNT(DISTINCT order_id); expected 0.',
'How do you verify a rewrite was correct?', 'Compare row grain, null counts, distinct keys and revenue before and after; both must reconcile to 578000.',
'First correctness, then plan inspection, then selective optimization, then revalidation.'),
L('SQL analytics capstone','Deliver repeatable, stakeholder-ready SQL outputs.',
'An analyst must supply business context, definitions, reproducible queries and evidence—not only a screenshot of a chart.',
'Begin with a metric contract (completed gross revenue, completed orders, profit). Build a validated fact extract, create group and time views, reconcile them, and document caveats such as the tiny illustrative dataset.',
"WITH fact AS (\n SELECT order_id,region,quantity*unit_price AS revenue,\n        quantity*(unit_price-unit_cost) AS profit\n FROM orders WHERE status='Completed'\n)\nSELECT region, COUNT(*) AS orders,\n SUM(revenue) AS revenue, SUM(profit) AS profit\nFROM fact GROUP BY region ORDER BY revenue DESC;",
'region | orders | revenue | profit\nEast   | 2      | 316000  | 66000\nWest   | 2      | 190000  | 45000\nNorth  | 1      | 72000   | 24000',
'Deliver a README with business grain, transformations, reconciliation checks and limitations.',
'Do not treat this six-row illustrative sample as evidence about real customer behavior.',
'Which two totals must reconcile across all regions?', 'Revenue = 578000 and profit = 135000 on completed orders.',
'What should you hand off to the dashboard developer?', 'Named queries, column dictionary, join keys, KPI definitions, validation outputs and a refresh assumption.',
'Define → transform → calculate → validate → communicate; keep the script versioned.')
]

EXCEL = [
L('Workbook fundamentals and analyst workflow','Navigate and structure a reproducible workbook.',
'An operations analyst receives daily order exports. A clear workbook structure reduces formula mistakes and lets a colleague audit the numbers.',
'Rows hold records, columns hold attributes, and cells hold values or formulas. Separate RawData, Calculations and Dashboard sheets; use one row per order, true dates, no merged data headers, and a clear source/date note.',
"RawData!A1:J3\nOrderID | Date       | Customer | Region | Product | Qty | Price | Cost  | Status\n1001    | 2026-01-05 | Amina    | East   | Laptop  | 2   | 150000| 120000| Completed\n1002    | 2026-01-08 | Kola     | West   | Mouse   | 5   | 8000  | 5000  | Completed",
'One header row; each order occupies one row; do not use subtotals inside the source table.',
'Name raw input tabs and preserve original file versions; do not edit imported records invisibly.',
'Merged headers, blank separator rows, and numbers stored as text break filters and PivotTables.',
'What is the grain of this input?', 'One row represents one order; OrderID is a unique key in this sample.',
'How should the workbook be separated?', 'RawData for source rows, Calculations for validated measures, Dashboard for user-facing visuals.',
'Use clean tabular source data; label units and grain; avoid mixing input with presentation.', 'table'),
L('Excel Tables and validation','Make calculations expand automatically as transactions grow.',
'Order logs grow daily. A manual formula range stops at yesterday’s last row, whereas a Table can extend calculated columns and references.',
'Ctrl+T creates an Excel Table with headers, filters, and structured references. Add a Revenue column with =[@Qty]*[@Price]; Excel fills it down the Table. Data Validation can restrict Status to permitted values.',
"Name the table: Sales\nRevenue column formula: =[@Qty]*[@Price]\nStatus validation list: Completed,Returned\nFor OrderID 1001: 2 * 150000 = 300000",
'OrderID | Qty | Price  | Revenue\n1001    | 2   | 150000 | 300000\n1002    | 5   | 8000   | 40000',
'Use Tables instead of fixed A2:A100 ranges in models that receive new rows.',
'Data Validation improves entry quality, but imported data can still violate rules; audit it too.',
'What happens after a seventh row is entered in Sales?', 'Structured calculated columns generally extend automatically to the new Table row.',
'How should you calculate cost per order?', 'Add =[@Qty]*[@Cost] in a LineCost calculated column.',
'Tables scale; structured references reduce broken formulas; validation is preventive, not sufficient.'),
L('References and core formulas','Compute repeatable sales metrics without broken references.',
'Finance often asks for tax, targets and profit; formulas must handle fill-down correctly and preserve fixed assumptions.',
'Relative references move when copied. An absolute reference such as $M$2 never moves. SUM aggregates, ROUND controls display precision, and IFERROR handles anticipated calculation errors without hiding all source defects.',
"Assume Sales[Revenue] has six gross order values.\n=SUM(Sales[Revenue])                 → 602000\n=SUMIFS(Sales[Revenue],Sales[Status],\"Completed\") → 578000\n=ROUND(578000*$M$2,2) with M2=7.5%  → 43350",
'Metric                  | Value\nAll-order gross sales   | 602000\nCompleted gross sales   | 578000\n7.5% hypothetical levy | 43350',
'Keep assumptions on an Inputs sheet, with cells named and referenced rather than hard-coded into each formula.',
'Using A2 instead of $A$2 for a single global rate changes the rate as the formula is copied.',
'Which formula computes profit on a Table row?', '=[@Qty]*([@Price]-[@Cost]); OrderID 1001 returns 60000.',
'Why is 602000 not the completed-sales KPI?', 'It includes the returned order 1003 (24000); filter status to Completed to obtain 578000.',
'Choose relative vs absolute carefully; clarify included records; reconcile derived measures.'),
L('IF, SUMIFS, XLOOKUP and legacy lookups','Apply business rules and join small reference lists.',
'Order reviews require statuses, thresholds and product categories from a lookup table.',
'IF returns a branch; SUMIFS calculates numeric totals with multiple criteria. XLOOKUP matches a key and returns a related value in newer Excel; for Excel 2016 use INDEX/MATCH or VLOOKUP with exact-match FALSE. Guard against missing keys.',
"=IF([@Status]=\"Completed\",[@Revenue],0)\n=SUMIFS(Sales[Revenue],Sales[Status],\"Completed\",Sales[Region],\"East\")\nExcel 2016: =IFERROR(INDEX(Category[Group],MATCH([@Product],Category[Product],0)),\"Unmapped\")",
'Completed East sales: 316000.\nMapped example: Laptop → Electronics.\nUnknown product → Unmapped (not a fabricated category).',
'Always request exact matching for IDs; treat unmatched keys as a visible data-quality outcome.',
'VLOOKUP without FALSE can return approximate matches and silently misclassify products.',
'What result should an East+Completed SUMIFS produce?', '316000: orders 1001 and 1006.',
'What should an unmatched product map to?', 'Explicit Unmapped or an error review list, never a guessed category.',
'Define criteria precisely; prefer explicit exact-match lookups; surface missing mappings.'),
L('Cleaning and data preparation','Create a consistent source without losing provenance.',
'In real feeds, spaces, duplicates, invalid dates and inconsistent capitalization can fragment business categories.',
'TRIM removes surplus spaces, CLEAN removes nonprinting characters, PROPER standardizes names when appropriate, and COUNTIF can identify repeat IDs. Power Query preserves a replayable sequence of transformations.',
"=TRIM(A2)                  // \"  East  \" → \"East\"\n=COUNTIF(Sales[OrderID],[@OrderID])  // expected 1 per sample row\n=IF([@Status]=\"Returned\",\"Excluded from completed revenue\",\"Include\")",
'Status=Returned on OrderID 1003 → Excluded from completed revenue.\nDuplicates in mastery data: 0.',
'Retain a raw snapshot, document every transformation and test uniqueness after cleanup.',
'PROPER should not automatically change company acronyms or official names; get a business rule first.',
'Why preserve original source data?', 'To trace and reverse changes if a transformation produces a wrong figure.',
'Name a duplicate-control formula.', '=COUNTIF(Sales[OrderID],[@OrderID])>1 returns FALSE for all six training orders.',
'Clean systematically; keep audit trails; assess source quality before reporting.'),
L('Sorting, filters and quick analysis','Explore trends without corrupting row relationships.',
'Sales managers filter geography and status during daily reviews; accidental partial-range sorting can detach quantities from the wrong orders.',
'Table filtering hides unrelated rows without deleting them. SUMIFS respects explicit logical criteria, while SUBTOTAL can aggregate visible rows only. Keep source filters separate from final KPI definitions.',
"=SUBTOTAL(109,Sales[Revenue])\nAfter filtering Status=Completed and Region=West:\nVisible OrderIDs: 1002 and 1005",
'Visible West completed revenue: 40000 + 150000 = 190000.\nThe unfiltered all-status gross remains 602000.',
'Use slicers only after checking whether they apply to the intended Table or PivotTable.',
'Sorting a single column independently of the rest of a record destroys row integrity.',
'Which West orders qualify?', 'OrderIDs 1002 and 1005; both are Completed.',
'What is the difference between SUM and SUBTOTAL(109)?', 'SUM includes filtered-out values; SUBTOTAL function 109 excludes manually hidden and filtered-out rows.',
'Sort entire Tables; distinguish filtered view from business eligibility; use SUBTOTAL for visible-row summaries.'),
L('PivotTables, PivotCharts and percentage views','Aggregate repeated records into an interactive management summary.',
'A 1,000-row sales log is difficult to interpret row-by-row. A PivotTable groups regions, months and products without manual SUMIFS for every combination.',
'Insert a PivotTable from Sales, set Rows=Region, Values=Sum of Revenue, and Filters=Status. Refresh when the source changes. Show Values As % of Grand Total uses the current Pivot context, not an all-time static denominator.',
"Pivot configuration:\nRows: Region\nValues: Sum of Revenue\nFilter: Status = Completed\nShow Values As: % of Grand Total",
'Region | Revenue | % of completed total\nEast   | 316000  | 54.67%\nWest   | 190000  | 32.87%\nNorth  | 72000   | 12.46%',
'Check source filters and grand totals before formatting a PivotTable as a polished chart.',
'Sum of a percentage column is usually not a weighted rate; PivotTables require a correct denominator.',
'Which region has the highest share?', 'East at about 54.67% of the 578000 completed-sales total.',
'Why might the Pivot total not reflect a newly added row?', 'The PivotTable may need refreshing; check source Table coverage and Refresh.',
'Pivots aggregate by dimensions; always verify filters, denominators and refresh behavior.'),
L('Charts and visual communication','Select graphics based on decision and metric shape.',
'Stakeholders should identify what changed and where to act within seconds.',
'Bars compare discrete regions; a line displays time order; scatterplots explain relationships. Use direct labels, consistent units and zero-based axes for magnitude comparisons. Separate a revenue line from a percentage-margin line unless clearly dual-labelled.',
"Build two charts from completed sales:\nChart A: horizontal bars — East 316000, West 190000, North 72000\nChart B: month line — Jan 340000, Feb 238000",
'The region bar lengths reflect 316k > 190k > 72k.\nThe monthly line falls from 340k to 238k (-30%).',
'Choose chart type from the question first, then format for accessibility and color-blind readability.',
'Truncated bar axes visually exaggerate differences; charts without denominator labels mislead.',
'What chart shows the relative size of three regions?', 'A horizontal bar chart with the axis starting at zero.',
'What is the right chart for revenue over months?', 'A chronologically sorted line chart with a year-month key.',
'Chart by purpose, not novelty; label measures and periods; avoid visual distortion.', 'table'),
L('Dashboard engineering and interaction','Design a one-page decision flow from trusted KPIs.',
'A dashboard needs a defined audience, a small set of critical KPIs and clear interactions rather than a wall of charts.',
'Use a header with the reporting period, KPI row, trend and breakdown visuals, and source/refresh notes. If slicers affect only some visuals, make this explicit. Keep raw data and calculations out of the presentation layer.',
"Suggested layout (completed orders only):\n[Revenue 578000] [Profit 135000] [Orders 5] [Margin 23.36%]\n[Monthly revenue trend] [Revenue by region]\n[Status selector] [Refresh date / metric definitions]",
'Completed revenue 578000; profit 135000; gross margin 135000/578000 = 23.36%.',
'Test every slicer combination and compare the cards against a PivotTable or SUMIFS control.',
'Dashboard KPIs may disagree if one uses all orders and another excludes returned orders.',
'What should appear next to a gross-margin KPI?', 'The calculation definition and the current reporting period; profit divided by completed revenue.',
'How can an end user reset interactions?', 'Provide clear slicer reset instructions or a documented reset control, and test it.',
'Put decisions before visuals; preserve metric consistency; test interactions and refresh notes.'),
L('Excel portfolio capstone','Deliver an audit-friendly retail operations dashboard.',
'A finished analytical workbook should survive a new batch of rows and allow another analyst to reproduce every figure.',
'Build Sales as a Table, add revenue/profit calculations, write source checks, create monthly and regional Pivots, then a restrained dashboard. Record assumptions and verify month totals against the transaction data.',
"Acceptance controls:\nCompleted Order Count: =COUNTIF(Sales[Status],\"Completed\")\nCompleted Revenue: =SUMIFS(Sales[Revenue],Sales[Status],\"Completed\")\nCompleted Profit: SUMIFS over a calculated Profit column",
'Expected: Orders=5; Revenue=578000; Profit=135000; Returned order excluded.',
'Include a compact QA sheet with key counts, totals, source version and refresh procedure.',
'Do not submit an attractive dashboard with broken structured references or unrefreshed Pivots.',
'What must update when a seventh order arrives?', 'The Table, formulas, Pivot sources/refresh and dashboards should reflect the new row.',
'Which three checks should always reconcile?', 'Completed order count=5, completed revenue=578000, and profit=135000 for the sample.',
'Build once, refresh repeatedly, audit with an independent control.')
]

POWERBI = [
L('Power BI Desktop and business questions','Translate a retail brief into a report plan.',
'Power BI is valuable when stakeholders need governed measures, reusable relationships, and interactive slicing beyond a static spreadsheet.',
'Power Query ingests/transforms, the semantic model stores tables and relationships, DAX evaluates measures, and report visuals query the model. Identify fact grain, dimensions, data refresh cadence and who can access the result before creating charts.',
"Model design brief:\nFactSales: one row per order (six training rows)\nDimensions: DimDate, DimCustomer, DimProduct, DimRegion\nKPI contract: completed revenue, completed profit, completed order count",
'Accepted source controls: six total rows; five Completed; completed revenue 578000.',
'Write metric contracts before DAX; include units, transaction inclusion rules and rounding.',
'A visual can look correct while using implicit sums on the wrong transaction statuses.',
'What is the report’s fact grain?', 'One order in the example; production systems may require an order-line grain.',
'What is the business definition of revenue here?', 'SUM of quantity × unit price for records with Status = Completed.',
'Business question → grain → model → measure → visual → validation.', 'table'),
L('Power Query M and repeatable cleanup','Build a refreshable transformation pipeline.',
'Repeated CSV imports arrive with text-typed numbers, inconsistent columns and malformed dates. The refresh process should apply the same transformations reliably.',
'Power Query records ordered Applied Steps. Set explicit types, trim controlled dimensions, reject duplicate order IDs only after investigating source truth, and use transformations instead of manually editing a few preview rows.',
"let\n Source = Csv.Document(File.Contents(\"mastery_orders.csv\"),\n   [Delimiter=\",\", Encoding=65001, QuoteStyle=QuoteStyle.Csv]),\n Headers = Table.PromoteHeaders(Source,[PromoteAllScalars=true]),\n Typed = Table.TransformColumnTypes(Headers,\n  {{\"quantity\",Int64.Type},{\"unit_price\",type number},\n   {\"unit_cost\",type number},{\"order_date\",type date}})\nin Typed",
'Power Query preview: 6 rows; quantity is Whole Number; unit_price is Decimal Number; order_date is Date.',
'Use parameters or managed connectors for production file paths, not hard-coded local paths.',
'Automatic Changed Type may misinterpret day/month locale; explicitly validate dates and currencies.',
'Why does changing column type matter?', 'DAX aggregation and relationship logic need proper numeric/date types, not text.',
'How do you surface bad input rows?', 'Use column quality/error filters and separate exception outputs rather than quietly deleting errors.',
'Applied Steps are repeatable; set types deliberately; preserve error evidence.'),
L('Star schema and relationships','Prevent ambiguous filters and incorrect double-counting.',
'Business slicers for customers, regions and time should filter a consistent fact table without causing duplicated totals.',
'Keep transaction columns in FactSales, attributes in dimensions. Make one-to-many relationships from dimension unique key to fact foreign key; default to single-direction filtering. A calendar dimension provides contiguous dates and accurate time intelligence.',
"DimCustomer[customer_id] (1) → (*) FactSales[customer_id]\nDimDate[Date] (1) → (*) FactSales[order_date]\nDimProduct[product_id] (1) → (*) FactSales[product_id]\nFactSales has 6 unique order IDs in the sample.",
'Cross-filter Region=East and Status=Completed → revenue 316000.',
'Keep relationships single direction until an explicit model requirement proves otherwise.',
'Many-to-many keys and indiscriminate bidirectional filters can cause ambiguous paths or incorrect totals.',
'Why must DimCustomer key be unique?', 'To guarantee a predictable one-to-many relationship and stable filter propagation.',
'How can you check the model after relationships?', 'Compare FactSales row counts and a completed-sales measure against independent source totals.',
'Facts hold events; dimensions describe them; relationship direction governs filters.', 'table'),
L('DAX measures and evaluation context','Build reusable completed-sales KPIs.',
'A table visual and a KPI card must use the same revenue definition while respecting each report selection.',
'Measures are evaluated at query time in filter context (slicers, rows, filters). SUMX iterates rows to compute quantity × price; CALCULATE modifies filter context. A calculated column computes per row at refresh; a measure responds to current selections.',
"Revenue :=\nCALCULATE(\n SUMX(FactSales, FactSales[quantity]*FactSales[unit_price]),\n FactSales[status] = \"Completed\"\n)\n\nProfit :=\nCALCULATE(\n SUMX(FactSales, FactSales[quantity]*(FactSales[unit_price]-FactSales[unit_cost])),\n FactSales[status] = \"Completed\"\n)",
'Unfiltered slicers: Revenue=578000; Profit=135000.\nRegion slicer East: Revenue=316000; Profit=66000.',
'Prefer explicit measures with clear names; show the difference between row context and filter context when teaching SUMX.',
'SUM(FactSales[quantity]*FactSales[unit_price]) is invalid DAX; use SUMX for an expression.',
'What does CALCULATE change?', 'Filter context, here limiting the measure to Completed records.',
'What is the revenue when filtering East?', '316000, the sum of East completed orders 1001 and 1006.',
'Row vs filter context; CALCULATE modifies filters; iterators evaluate row expressions.'),
L('Date tables and time intelligence','Compute period changes on a valid model calendar.',
'Managers compare this month with previous months; string dates and missing calendar rows make time-intelligence results unreliable.',
'A marked Date table has a contiguous unique date column, related actively to fact dates. CALCULATE can shift filter contexts through date tables. Close month logic and partial periods require explicit business definitions.',
"DimDate = CALENDAR(DATE(2026,1,1),DATE(2026,12,31))\n\nRevenue PM :=\nCALCULATE([Revenue], DATEADD(DimDate[Date],-1,MONTH))\n\nMoM % := DIVIDE([Revenue]-[Revenue PM],[Revenue PM])",
'January 2026 revenue=340000. February=238000.\nFebruary MoM change=(238000-340000)/340000=-30.00%.',
'Mark DimDate as a Date table and use the date column from it in time visuals.',
'Using FactSales[order_date] text or a noncontiguous date list defeats reliable period calculations.',
'What is the January prior-month value in this two-month sample?', 'BLANK unless a prior December context and matching data are provided.',
'Why use DIVIDE instead of /?', 'DIVIDE handles zero or blank denominators safely and returns BLANK by default.',
'Date model first; shifted filter context next; compare like-for-like complete periods.'),
L('Visualization and report UX','Build a visual hierarchy for fast decisions.',
'Report readers need to spot variance and drill into drivers, not decode twelve disconnected charts.',
'Use a few KPI cards, one monthly trend, one region comparison, a clear slicer zone, and short definitions. Use conditional formatting only for defined thresholds. Verify accessibility (contrast, titles, keyboard use, and alt text where supported).',
"Executive page wireframe:\n[Completed Revenue 578000] [Profit 135000] [Margin 23.36%]\n[Monthly line: Jan 340000 → Feb 238000]\n[Region bars: East 316000 | West 190000 | North 72000]",
'Sample output communicates a -30% month-over-month decline, not a growth claim.',
'Use native Power BI visuals where possible; avoid dense ornamentation and false precision.',
'A red card is not meaningful without a documented threshold or previous-period comparator.',
'Which chart best compares three regions?', 'A sorted horizontal bar chart with values starting at zero.',
'Which supporting metadata should appear?', 'Reporting period, source system, refresh time, units and metric definitions.',
'Build page narrative from KPI → trend → drivers → action.', 'table'),
L('Slicers, drillthrough and navigation','Provide controlled exploration without losing context.',
'Operational managers often need to click a region, inspect customer-level drivers and return to the same overview.',
'Slicers apply filter context to connected visuals; Edit Interactions overrides defaults. Drillthrough passes categorical filters into a detail page. Bookmarks can capture data state, display state or both, so reset controls must be tested.',
"Suggested interaction test:\n1. Select Region = West.\n2. Revenue KPI must show 190000.\n3. Drill through to customers Kola and Idris.\n4. Return and Reset; KPI must return to 578000.",
'West revenue=190000 (orders 1002 and 1005). Reset revenue=578000.',
'Test navigation on desktop and Power BI mobile layouts separately.',
'A bookmark that captures data filters unintentionally can reset slicers the user wanted to preserve.',
'What does a drillthrough filter convey?', 'The selected region/customer category to the destination detail page.',
'How do you validate Reset?', 'Change two slicers, click Reset, verify every intended visual and KPI matches default state.',
'Interactions modify filter context; navigation must preserve clear user expectations.'),
L('Intermediate DAX and context control','Calculate shares and ranks with explicit denominators.',
'Leaders ask which region contributes the most and what a customer’s share is within selected periods.',
'CALCULATE can remove specific filter columns; ALL/REMOVEFILTERS affect denominator context. RANKX compares row measures over a defined dimension set. Keep date filters when calculating region shares if you want period-relative shares.',
"Region Share % :=\nDIVIDE(\n [Revenue],\n CALCULATE([Revenue], REMOVEFILTERS(DimRegion[region]))\n)\n\nRegion Rank :=\nRANKX(ALL(DimRegion[region]),[Revenue],,DESC,Dense)",
'East=54.67%, West=32.87%, North=12.46% of the 578000 completed total.\nRanks: East 1, West 2, North 3.',
'Write denominator semantics into measure descriptions: overall, selected, or period-specific.',
'ALL on the entire fact table can remove time/status context and inflate denominators.',
'Why use REMOVEFILTERS on only the region column?', 'To preserve the selected date and other compatible filters while computing regional share.',
'What rank does North receive?', '3 under descending revenue ranking.',
'Write explicit denominators; constrain filter removal; validate measure behavior under slicers.'),
L('Power BI Service, sharing and refresh','Deliver the report securely to its actual audience.',
'Publishing is not complete if users lack access, the data stays stale, or confidential rows leak between teams.',
'Publish the semantic model and report to a managed workspace. Set refresh credentials or gateway as needed, review licensing/workspace policies, configure row-level security where required, and test as a viewer. A successful refresh does not guarantee correct metrics.',
"Deployment checklist:\n□ Credentials and refresh schedule documented\n□ Role-based access tested\n□ One-page definitions and source notes included\n□ Completed revenue reconciles to 578000 before publishing\n□ Failed-refresh escalation owner assigned",
'QA acceptance: authorized viewer can open the report; sample KPI values remain consistent.',
'Check semantic model permissions as well as report sharing; security depends on access configuration.',
'Assuming a hidden report page is a security boundary; it is not.',
'When is a gateway relevant?', 'When Power BI Service must access a supported on-premises data source that is not directly cloud accessible.',
'What should be tested after publication?', 'Data refresh, viewer permissions, RLS roles (if configured), navigation, filters and KPIs.',
'Secure the model; schedule refresh; test viewer experience; preserve metric integrity.'),
L('Power BI portfolio capstone','Present a governed retail executive dashboard.',
'A reusable semantic model and a concise insight narrative demonstrate job-ready analytical judgment.',
'Import CSV through Power Query, model dimensions and dates, create explicit DAX measures, design overview/detail pages, test slicers, and write a short QA note. Separate demonstration values from real-world claims.',
"Required measures and controls:\n[Revenue] = 578000\n[Profit] = 135000\n[Orders Completed] = 5\n[Margin %] = DIVIDE([Profit],[Revenue]) = 23.36%\n[February MoM %] = -30.00%",
'Accepted training-model totals: Revenue 578000; Profit 135000; Completed orders 5.',
'Submit a .pbix report (where available), screenshot, definitions, and verification worksheet.',
'A beautiful visual with misconfigured relationships is not a successful model.',
'Which measures reconcile to source detail?', 'Revenue=578000; profit=135000; count=5.',
'What constitutes adequate handover?', 'Model diagram, measure dictionary, slicer QA, refresh/security notes and a concise business narrative.',
'Source → Power Query → star model → DAX → visuals → secure deployment → QA.')
]

PYTHON = [
L('Python setup, files and analyst mindset','Load a dataset reproducibly and inspect its structure.',
'Analysts use Python when work must be repeatable, combine multiple files, or scale beyond manual clicking. The first objective is a clean, runnable environment.',
'Python executes statements in order, stores objects in variables, and imports reusable packages. Use a virtual environment, pin dependencies, keep raw files separate, and confirm the working directory before reading CSV data.',
"from pathlib import Path\nimport pandas as pd\npath = Path('mastery_orders.csv')\norders = pd.read_csv(path, parse_dates=['order_date'])\nprint(orders.shape)\nprint(orders['status'].value_counts().to_dict())",
"(6, 12)\n{'Completed': 5, 'Returned': 1}",
'Use python -m venv .venv and a requirements file. Do not embed credentials in notebooks.',
'FileNotFoundError often means the working directory is wrong; inspect Path.cwd() and the path first.',
'What does orders.shape report?', 'The number of rows and columns as a tuple: (6,12) for the supplied file.',
'How can you avoid treating dates as arbitrary strings?', "Pass parse_dates=['order_date'] in read_csv or validate with pd.to_datetime.",
'Create environment → read input → inspect rows/types → validate before transforming.'),
L('Variables, types and control flow','Translate a calculation rule into correct Python logic.',
'Reporting calculations rely on precise numeric types and conditions; errors can turn a returned order into recognized sales.',
'int, float, str, bool and None model different values. Conditions are evaluated before a result is added. Use Decimal when exact decimal-currency arithmetic is required; floats can produce rounding surprises.',
"orders = [\n {'id': 1001, 'qty': 2, 'price': 150000, 'status':'Completed'},\n {'id': 1003, 'qty': 3, 'price': 8000, 'status':'Returned'}\n]\nvalue = sum(o['qty'] * o['price']\n  for o in orders if o['status']=='Completed')\nprint(value)",
'300000 (returned order 1003 is excluded).',
'Prefer descriptive variable names and assertions for assumptions, e.g., assert amount >= 0.',
'Using = inside a condition is a syntax error; use == for equality. Avoid comparing text values to numbers.',
'What is the value of 3 * 8000?', '24000, but that returned order does not contribute to completed revenue.',
'How would you protect against a missing status value?', "Use o.get('status')=='Completed'; absent status fails the inclusion test.",
'Types determine valid operations; test conditions explicitly; keep business rules readable.'),
L('Functions, collections and reusable scripts','Write one calculation once and reuse it safely.',
'Analysts often repeat net-sales and margin logic across reports; functions prevent inconsistent copies.',
'A function receives parameters, evaluates a body, and returns a value. A list keeps ordered records; a dictionary maps named attributes. Type hints communicate contracts but do not validate values at runtime.',
"def revenue(quantity: int, unit_price: float) -> float:\n    if quantity < 0 or unit_price < 0:\n        raise ValueError('Negative input is not expected')\n    return quantity * unit_price\nprint(revenue(2, 150000))\nprint(revenue(5, 8000))",
'300000\n40000',
'Keep business rules in tested functions; keep file input/output at the edges of the script.',
'Mutable default arguments such as def f(items=[]) can leak state between calls.',
'Why return instead of only print?', 'Returned values can be reused in calculations, unit tests and reports.',
'What happens with revenue(-1,8000)?', 'It raises ValueError because the function rejects unexpected negative quantities.',
'Small functions are easier to test; keep inputs explicit and outputs predictable.'),
L('pandas selection and filtering','Filter the correct transactions before aggregation.',
'A pandas DataFrame brings SQL-like filtering to CSV data, allowing consistent calculations in automated pipelines.',
'A DataFrame is a two-dimensional labelled table. A Boolean mask marks eligible rows; .loc selects rows and columns by labels. Vectorised operations generally avoid slow Python-level iteration.',
"import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv('mastery_orders.csv')\ncompleted = df.loc[df['status'].eq('Completed')].copy()\ncompleted['revenue'] = completed['quantity'] * completed['unit_price']\nprint(len(completed), int(completed['revenue'].sum()))",
'5 578000',
'Use .loc and .copy() when creating a filtered DataFrame to make intent explicit.',
'Do not use `and` between pandas Series masks; use `&` with parentheses: (a) & (b).',
'How many rows qualify as Completed?', '5; returned order 1003 is excluded by the Boolean mask.',
'How do you select just East completed orders?', "df.loc[df['status'].eq('Completed') & df['region'].eq('East')] returns orders 1001 and 1006.",
'Filter using Boolean masks; vectorize arithmetic; validate row counts.'),
L('Cleaning, nulls, typing and joins','Protect an analytics pipeline against faulty imports.',
'Noisy files often carry blank customer IDs, mixed formats and duplicate keys. Failures should be reported, not silently discarded.',
'Use .isna() for NULL checks, .duplicated() for repeated keys and .merge(validate=...) to enforce join cardinality. Convert types deliberately with pd.to_numeric and pd.to_datetime; coerce errors only when you keep an exception count.',
"import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv('mastery_orders.csv')\nprint(int(df['order_id'].duplicated().sum()))\nprint(int(df['customer_id'].isna().sum()))\nassert df['order_id'].is_unique\nassert df['quantity'].ge(0).all()",
'0 duplicate OrderIDs\n0 missing customer IDs\nAssertions pass for the training file.',
'Check duplicates and missing IDs both before and after merges; use validate="many_to_one" for fact→dimension lookups.',
'A merge on duplicate lookup keys can multiply transaction rows and inflate sales.',
'What does validate="many_to_one" mean?', 'Many fact rows can match one dimension key, but the dimension key must be unique.',
'When should to_numeric(errors="coerce") be used?', 'Only alongside a review of rows converted to NaN, not as a silent data-loss shortcut.',
'Validate keys, types and joins; produce an exception report alongside cleaned data.'),
L('groupby, pivot and business KPI reporting','Generate a clear, reproducible regional summary.',
'An automated weekly report needs the same measure definitions as the SQL and Power BI versions.',
'groupby creates category partitions and reduces each to aggregations. Avoid adding percentages as if they were additive measures; compute a ratio from aggregated numerator and denominator.',
"import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv('mastery_orders.csv')\nx = df.loc[df.status.eq('Completed')].copy()\nx['revenue'] = x.quantity * x.unit_price\nprint(x.groupby('region')['revenue'].sum().sort_values(ascending=False).to_dict())",
"{'East': 316000, 'West': 190000, 'North': 72000}",
'Use named aggregation and unit-tested metric functions in production reporting code.',
'groupby drops NULL grouping keys by default in pandas unless dropna=False is specified.',
'How do you calculate completed profit?', "Create x['profit']=x.quantity*(x.unit_price-x.unit_cost), then sum: 135000.",
'How do you confirm the regional totals?', 'Sum regional revenue totals; the result must equal 578000.',
'Filter correctly; group at the business grain; reconcile grouped sums.'),
L('Statistics and uncertainty','Interpret metrics without making unsupported causal claims.',
'Executives often ask whether an apparent change reflects a durable pattern or random variation; statistics clarify what data can and cannot establish.',
'Mean is sum/count, median is the middle observation after sorting, standard deviation measures variation, and sampling affects uncertainty. Correlation describes association, not proof of causation. Six fabricated rows are useful for learning syntax, not inference.',
"values = [300000,40000,72000,150000,16000]\nimport statistics as stats\nprint(stats.mean(values))\nprint(stats.median(values))\nprint(min(values), max(values))",
'115600\n72000\n16000 300000',
'Communicate sample size, metric definition and any selection bias alongside a summary.',
'Using the mean as a typical order is misleading when there is a large 300000 order; compare median.',
'Which statistic is more resistant to outliers?', 'Median: 72000 in this five-order completed sample.',
'Can you infer nationwide sales growth from these six rows?', 'No. The dataset is intentionally illustrative and not a representative population sample.',
'Describe distributions and limitations; avoid unsupported causal or population claims.'),
L('NumPy vectors and matrix intuition','Recognize vectorized calculations and matrix dimensions.',
'Forecasting and machine learning depend on efficient numeric arrays; matrix dimensions explain common shape errors.',
'A vector is an ordered numeric array; a matrix has rows and columns. Elementwise multiplication pairs corresponding positions; dot products sum pairwise products. A feature matrix X commonly has rows=observations and columns=features.',
"import numpy as np\nqty = np.array([2,5,4])\nprice = np.array([150000,8000,18000])\nprint((qty*price).tolist())\nprint(int(qty @ price))\nprint(np.column_stack([qty,price]).shape)",
'[300000, 40000, 72000]\n412000\n(3, 2)',
'Vectorise calculations where possible, but confirm array alignment and units before trusting speed gains.',
'Broadcasting may silently stretch arrays; use shape checks instead of relying on assumptions.',
'What does an array shape of (3,2) mean?', 'Three observations and two numeric features per observation.',
'What is the dot product of qty and price?', '412000, the sum of the three example line revenues.',
'Array shape matters; multiply elementwise for row totals; dot product sums them.'),
L('Time series, charts and testable forecasts','Create a baseline with transparent limitations.',
'Forecasting decisions require a benchmark and careful time-aware validation, rather than presenting a clever model as automatically accurate.',
'Group by chronological month, create a baseline such as previous-month value, and compare predicted vs actual on later unseen periods. Data leakage occurs when future observations enter training features or preprocessing.',
"import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv('mastery_orders.csv',parse_dates=['order_date'])\nx = df[df.status.eq('Completed')].copy()\nx['revenue'] = x.quantity*x.unit_price\nmonthly = x.groupby(x.order_date.dt.to_period('M'))['revenue'].sum()\nprint(monthly.to_dict())",
"{Period('2026-01', 'M'): 340000, Period('2026-02', 'M'): 238000}",
'For a real forecast, gather many more periods, define a holdout window and compare against a naive baseline.',
'Two months cannot support a reliable seasonal forecast; never advertise predictive accuracy from this demo.',
'What is the naive forecast for February based on January?', '340000, the previous-month completed revenue.',
'What is the February absolute forecast error for that baseline?', 'abs(238000-340000)=102000.',
'Time-aware splits prevent leakage; compare with a baseline; disclose uncertainty.'),
L('Python analytics capstone','Package a repeatable, independently verified report.',
'Professional scripts deliver a consistent output and clear failure messages when inputs change.',
'Use functions for load, validate, transform, aggregate and export. Include small unit tests, fixed dependency versions, a README and documented schema. Reconcile totals against the SQL workbook control.',
"import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv('mastery_orders.csv')\nassert df.order_id.is_unique\nx = df[df.status.eq('Completed')].copy()\nx['revenue']=x.quantity*x.unit_price\nx['profit']=x.quantity*(x.unit_price-x.unit_cost)\nsummary = x.groupby('region')[['revenue','profit']].sum()\nassert x.revenue.sum()==578000\nassert x.profit.sum()==135000\nprint(summary.loc['East'].to_dict())",
"{'revenue': 316000, 'profit': 66000}",
'Add automated test cases for empty data, missing required fields and duplicate identifiers.',
'Notebook outputs saved after a different execution order are not proof of reproducibility; run from a clean kernel.',
'What should fail the capstone build?', 'Missing required columns, duplicated order IDs or totals that violate reconciliation controls.',
'What should the README specify?', 'How to install, where to place input files, expected schema, run command, metric definitions and known limitations.',
'Package source + tests + readme + validated outputs, not only notebook screenshots.')
]

AI = [
L('AI literacy, analytics tasks and limits','Separate language generation from verifiable computation.',
'Analysts can use AI to outline hypotheses and draft code quickly, but confident prose is not evidence that a number is correct.',
'Large language models predict plausible next tokens. External tools or an actual dataset engine are required for reproducible computation; prompt instructions alone do not grant access to the data. Distinguish summarization, retrieval, predictive modelling and deterministic SQL.',
"Prompt: 'Plan an analysis of mastery_orders.csv.\nIdentify the target KPI, required columns, risky assumptions,\nand validation steps. Do not invent sales totals.'",
'Expected response outline: business question → columns → inclusion rule → reproducible calculation → validation. Verified completed revenue = 578000 by separate code, not by prose alone.',
'Use AI to propose a workflow; execute critical calculations in SQL, Excel, Python or Power BI.',
'An LLM that claims it "checked the data" without actually using a tool has not verified it.',
'What is the difference between a plan and a computed KPI?', 'A plan describes steps; a computed KPI comes from executing the defined calculation on actual rows.',
'What source control validates a claimed 578000?', 'Re-run a Completed-only sum in SQL/Excel/Python and inspect six input records.',
'AI drafts; engines calculate; analysts validate.'),
L('Prompt engineering as a mini specification','Ask for testable and bounded analysis outputs.',
'A vague prompt such as "analyse my sales" produces generic prose, whereas a specification defines the business question and acceptance tests.',
'An effective prompt contains role, objective, input schema, inclusion/exclusion rules, output format and independent checks. Use small few-shot examples for complicated labelling; iteration should reduce ambiguity, not replace testing.',
"Prompt: 'Act as a retail analyst. Given fields order_id,\nstatus, quantity, unit_price and region, draft SQLite SQL\nthat sums quantity*unit_price only when status=Completed.\nReturn SQL, expected columns, and a reconciliation query.\nUse no invented columns or values.'",
'Expected SQL structure: WHERE status=\'Completed\'; SUM(quantity*unit_price); GROUP BY region. Verified groups: East 316000, West 190000, North 72000.',
'Treat acceptance criteria like software tests: expected columns, allowed tables and result reconciliation.',
'Without specifying SQL dialect, AI may suggest unsupported DATE_TRUNC or TOP syntax for SQLite.',
'What should be stated about the data schema?', 'Exact column names, types, grain and available tables.',
'What is an appropriate independent check?', 'An ungrouped Completed-only revenue query must equal the sum of grouped regions.',
'Specify input + rules + expected format + verification; iterate on reproducible errors.'),
L('Privacy, safety and responsible AI','Choose what can be shared with an AI service.',
'Customers, employee records and proprietary transactions may be confidential; disclosure must follow organizational policy and law.',
'Classify data by sensitivity, mask or aggregate identifiers, review provider retention/settings and workspace access rules, and keep confidential datasets in approved environments. Human review is needed for bias, erroneous inferences and disclosure risk.',
"Safe analysis handoff (synthetic):\nregion | completed_orders | revenue\nEast   | 2                | 316000\nWest   | 2                | 190000\nNorth  | 1                | 72000\nNo phone numbers, customer emails or credentials included.",
'Expected: an AI-ready aggregated summary with no personal identifiers. Sample values are fictional training data.',
'Use the minimum data needed; for sensitive material require explicitly approved company tools and data handling.',
'Replacing names with IDs alone may not anonymize rare or linkable personal records.',
'Can a raw customer export be pasted into a public assistant?', 'Not unless the organization permits it and privacy/security requirements are satisfied.',
'What should happen if a prompt requests an access token?', 'Never expose secrets; use approved secret-management processes and redesign the workflow.',
'Minimize data; obey policies; verify bias and security risks before sharing.'),
L('AI-supported Excel and Power Query','Generate formulas while validating workbook behavior.',
'AI can explain formula options, but a workbook still must use supported functions and the correct source table.',
'Provide column names and Excel version. Ask for a formula, explanation and edge-case tests. Apply it in Excel and compare a manual check; do not accept a generated formula merely because the syntax looks plausible.',
"Prompt: 'For Excel 2016 Table Sales with Quantity, Price,\nStatus and Region, give a formula for completed East revenue\nand explain each criterion. Avoid functions newer than 2016.'\n\nExpected: =SUMIFS(Sales[Revenue],Sales[Status],\"Completed\",Sales[Region],\"East\")",
'When Sales[Revenue]=[@Quantity]*[@Price], expected East completed revenue=316000.',
'Provide the actual Excel version; request a legacy-compatible option and test 0-row scenarios.',
'XLOOKUP is not available in Excel 2016; suggest INDEX/MATCH or VLOOKUP exact-match.',
'How do you validate the AI formula?', 'Filter completed East orders 1001 and 1006 and manually sum 300000+16000.',
'How should a formula treat Returned orders?', 'Exclude them from the defined completed-revenue calculation.',
'AI proposes formulas; your workbook and independent checks establish correctness.'),
L('AI-assisted SQL with schema-grounded output','Prevent plausible but invalid queries.',
'Text generators may guess joins, columns or dialect-specific functions. Giving a schema changes the exercise from creative writing to constrained code review.',
'Attach an explicit CREATE TABLE schema or allowed-column list, request a read-only query, and specify SQLite. Review filters, NULL logic and group grain; execute it against the training database and inspect results.',
"Prompt: 'SQLite table orders(order_id,order_date,region,\nquantity,unit_price,status). Write read-only SQL for\ncompleted monthly revenue. Return query and validation\nquery. Do not create missing columns.'\n\nSELECT strftime('%Y-%m',order_date) month,\nSUM(quantity*unit_price) revenue\nFROM orders WHERE status='Completed'\nGROUP BY month ORDER BY month;",
'Expected months: 2026-01 = 340000; 2026-02 = 238000.',
'Ask AI to state its assumed grain, SQL dialect and every join key before producing complex queries.',
'A tool-generated query can run successfully but still count returned orders; execution success is not business correctness.',
'What makes a query schema-grounded?', 'Only columns and tables present in the documented dataset are used.',
'What separate validation detects excluded-status mistakes?', 'Run a Completed-only total check (578000) and compare monthly sum.',
'Ground prompts in schema; run generated code; reconcile independently.'),
L('AI-assisted Python and debugging','Use AI to draft code and explain failures, not hide them.',
'Python automation is valuable but copying code blindly creates silent errors and brittle scripts.',
'Give the traceback, minimal safe example and package versions. Ask for the smallest fix and a test. Prefer deterministic pandas checks that assert row counts and financial totals.',
"Prompt: 'Explain this pandas bug and fix it: \nmask = (df.status == \"Completed\") and (df.region == \"East\")\nReturn corrected code and expected number of rows.'\n\nmask = (df.status == 'Completed') & (df.region == 'East')\nprint(df.loc[mask].shape[0])",
'Expected row count = 2. East completed revenue = 316000.',
'Request unit tests and explainability for any generated transformation; diff edits before running.',
'Python `and` cannot combine pandas Series masks; use `&` with parentheses.',
'Why did `and` fail?', 'The truth value of an entire pandas Series is ambiguous.',
'How would you assert the result?', 'assert df.loc[mask].shape[0] == 2 after loading the known training dataset.',
'Debug with minimal examples; prefer smallest fixes; add regression tests.'),
L('AI-assisted DAX and semantic models','Check evaluation context before trusting DAX.',
'AI can suggest DAX formulas that look correct but change when slicers filter the model.',
'Provide the fact grain, table names, relationships, measure definitions and expected filter behavior. Test measures in card and matrix visuals; differentiate row context from filter context and assess CALCULATE filter transitions.',
"Prompt: 'FactSales has quantity, unit_price, status, region.\nWrite a DAX measure for Completed revenue that responds\nto Region slicers. Use SUMX and CALCULATE; explain context.'\n\nRevenue := CALCULATE(SUMX(FactSales,\n FactSales[quantity]*FactSales[unit_price]),\n FactSales[status] = \"Completed\")",
'All regions = 578000; Region East = 316000; West = 190000.',
'Ask for filter-context tests, not only a measure. Validate a grand total and at least two slicer combinations.',
'Removing all fact filters to fix a single result may erase legitimate date/region selections.',
'What does SUMX evaluate?', 'An expression row-by-row over the table in the current filter context.',
'How do you prove the measure responds to a slicer?', 'East must yield 316000, then clearing the slicer returns 578000.',
'Specify model context; explain filters; test measures under actual interactions.'),
L('Statistical reasoning, ML and forecast critique','Distinguish credible predictive evidence from seductive narratives.',
'Forecasts are high-impact because decision makers may act on them; AI-generated accuracy claims require a real test design.',
'Check baseline methods, time-split validation, leakage, sample size, evaluation metric and confidence intervals. A model needs sufficient data for seasonality; two training months only demonstrate calculation.',
"Evaluation brief:\nObserved January completed revenue: 340000\nObserved February completed revenue: 238000\nNaive February forecast (use Jan): 340000\nAbsolute error: |238000-340000| = 102000",
'This demonstrates an error computation, not valid forecasting performance or expected future sales.',
'Ask AI to find leakage risks and propose a baseline before recommending a complex model.',
'Mixing future target data into training features creates deceptively optimistic accuracy.',
'Can this two-month sample establish a seasonal trend?', 'No, it cannot support seasonality or a defensible generalized forecast.',
'What baseline can be used for a time series?', 'Previous period value, evaluated on chronologically held-out periods.',
'Baseline first; chronological holdout; bias/leakage review; uncertainty disclosures.'),
L('Automation, agents and human approval','Design repeatable workflows with safeguards.',
'Scheduled reporting can save analyst hours, but an automated agent must not silently overwrite records or send incorrect figures.',
'A safe flow has trigger → ingest → validate → calculate → compare controls → approval → publish → monitor. Use least-privilege access, idempotent processing, logs and retries; require human approval for sensitive external actions.',
"Workflow pseudo-logic:\nif duplicate_order_ids > 0: BLOCK\nelif completed_revenue != reconciled_total: BLOCK\nelse: CREATE_DRAFT_REPORT\nHUMAN_REVIEW → PUBLISH_TO_AUTHORISED_AUDIENCE",
'Expected controls on training data: 0 duplicate IDs; completed revenue 578000; workflow may advance to draft review.',
'Log source version, execution timestamp, check status and reviewer approval; alert when assumptions break.',
'Automatic publication without permission, source validation or rollback creates avoidable operational risk.',
'What does idempotent mean in this workflow?', 'Rerunning a task should not duplicate deliveries or mutate results repeatedly.',
'What triggers a stop?', 'Unresolved source defects, failed reconciliations, unavailable authorization, or unapproved sensitive action.',
'Automate deterministic checks; gate risky actions; retain logs and rollback options.'),
L('Governed AI analytics capstone','Deliver an independently checked AI-assisted analysis.',
'A credible AI portfolio submission shows how tools accelerated work without replacing analytical responsibility.',
'Define a real question, sanitize inputs, draft SQL/Excel/Python/DAX with AI, execute in an appropriate engine, reconcile to a gold control, and document the precise role AI played. Record limitations and a reviewer sign-off.',
"Deliverable evidence:\n1. Business question and synthetic input dictionary\n2. One clearly bounded AI prompt and its draft output\n3. Independently executed calculation + error checks\n4. Executive insight + privacy review\n5. Reproduction instructions and AI-use declaration",
'Accepted sample controls: Completed orders 5, revenue 578000, profit 135000. All claims must match the executed training dataset.',
'Create a side-by-side AI suggestion vs tested result table; highlight corrections openly.',
'Concealing invented figures, sensitive inputs or unreviewed code invalidates the demonstration.',
'How should a generated but unexecuted query be labelled?', 'Draft / unverified, never an actual measured output.',
'What justifies sign-off?', 'Correct computations, documented sources, tested assumptions, appropriate data handling, reproducibility and human review.',
'Prompt → draft → verify → document → communicate, under explicit privacy controls.')
]

